  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nThere are a few light showers lingering over parts of central South \nDakota early this morning (as of ~06Z). Expectations over the next \nfew hours are that showers will continue to move into the area, but \nwill dissipate shortly after moving in. By this afternoon, expect \nskies to be clear of rain showers, although cloudy skies may persist \ntoday. \n\nChances for rain will return on Sunday. A jet streak will develop in \nthe right entrance region of the departing upper-level trough to the \neast, providing the divergence aloft that will help with development \nof showers and storms. CAMs highlight two main areas for storm \ndevelopment early Sunday morning, one over north central and \nnortheastern South Dakota and the other over central to south \ncentral South Dakota. The latter area shows a bit more organization \nand better chances for storms, but severe potential has waned in \nthat area. Even heavy rain is looking less likely, down to about a \n10-30% chance to see a quarter of an inch through Sunday morning. \n\nAn upper-level trough to the west will help to develop another low \npressure center and associated frontal boundary set to track across \nthe region Monday. Along and ahead of this front additional chances \nfor rain are expected early Monday morning. Latest NBM probabilities \nshow a bit of an increase from 24 hours ago in the probability to \nreceive half an inch of rain. Through Monday evening, chances now \nsit at about 60-80% of half an inch (and 20-45% for one inch) over \nmost of the forecast area. Expectations for the highest totals are \nalong the North Dakota/South Dakota border. Euro and Canadian \nensembles are fairly in line with these same probabilities, but \ninterestingly the GEFS shows less than a 5% chance for half an inch \nof rain across the board. Perhaps the possible explanation for this \nseemingly extreme scenario is that the colder near-surface \nenvironment may limit deeper convection with this system. However \nwith nearly 40 knots of speed shear above the warm layer and the \npresence of the front, showers to weak thunderstorms will likely \nstill be able to form. At this point conditions do not appear \nfavorable for severe weather from these storms. \n\nAfter the passage of yesterday's front, temperatures will take a \ndrop into the 60s to low 70s over the weekend, about 5-10 degrees \nbelow normal for mid-September. This trend will continue into the \nstart of next week, with the exception of a brief warmup to near-\nnormal for central and north central South Dakota on Monday. Longer \nrange models show sings of a warmup by the end of next week to at \nleast near-normal temperatures across the forecast area.",
